# A RETRIEVED REGULATION IS EVIDENCE, SO ITS BYTES MUST NOT BE TRANSLATED.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set in this repository and nothing declared an
# exception, so a text file is stored LF and checked out CRLF on Windows.
# For source code that is invisible. For a committed snapshot of an
# official document it is not: `osha_1910_1000.xml` carries 4763 bare LFs
# and its sha256 travels in the generated ruleset's provenance, so a fresh
# Windows clone would hash 176334 CRLF-expanded bytes to a different digest
# than Linux CI hashes -- and the guard tying the shipped numbers to the
# committed regulation would go red on one platform for a reason with
# nothing to do with the regulation.
#
# NORMALISING THE HASH INSTEAD WOULD BE WRONG HERE, and that is the
# distinction worth keeping. `tools/build_regulatory_rulesets.py` DOES
# hash newline-normalised text, correctly: it is checking whether a
# GENERATED artefact is current, and a line ending is not a content edit.
# This file is not ours. Its digest answers "is this the document we read",
# so it has to be the document's own bytes.
src/openchem/chem/data/regulatory/sources/*.xml -text

# Published parameter files copied byte for byte from a paper's supplement, for the same reason:
# their READMEs record the supplement's own SHA-256, which CRLF expansion would change.
tests/fixtures/charges/schindler2021/SQE_CCD_gen_parameters.json -text
tests/fixtures/charges/geidl2015/B3LYP_6-311G_NPA.par -text
benchmarks/charges/models/schindler_sqe_results/*.json -text

# Clean-room QTPIE result files, whose hashes the pre-registration records.
benchmarks/charges/models/qtpie_cleanroom_results*.json -text
